(
  'IELTS writing band score: how your essay is really marked',
  'Understand how IELTS Writing is marked across Task 1 and Task 2, then use the four official criteria to target the weakest part of your essay.',
  'Learn how the IELTS writing band score is calculated for Task 1 and Task 2, with practical ways to use the four official criteria to improve.',
  'https://www.ieltsai.pro/blog/ielts-writing-band-score-how-your-essay-is-really-marked',
  'blog',
  $post$
  <p>You sit down after your IELTS exam, and a few days later your IELTS writing band score appears on your screen. Maybe it's a 6.0 when you needed a 6.5. Maybe it's a 7.0 when you were sure you'd written better than that. The number tells you nothing about what went wrong, which paragraph fell flat, or which single skill cost you a full band. That frustration is entirely avoidable, and this article is going to fix it.</p>
  <p>Your IELTS writing band score is not a mystery number assigned by instinct. It is the calculated average of four clearly defined criteria, each scored on the same 0, 9 scale, with specific descriptors that tell you exactly what Band 6, Band 7, and Band 8 look like in practice. Once you understand the framework, you can read your own writing and estimate where it sits before you even sit the exam. You can identify the single weakest criterion and fix it first, instead of practicing everything at once and improving nothing efficiently.</p>
  <p>If you want to see this scoring framework applied to your own writing right now, <a href="/blog/how-an-ai-ielts-platform-can-fast-track-your-band-score">IELTS Ai Pro</a> lets you submit a Task 1 or Task 2 essay and receive an instant AI-estimated band score broken down across all <a href="https://ielts.org/news-and-insights/ielts-writing-band-descriptors-and-key-assessment-criteria" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">four official criteria</a>. It is a fast way to get a personalized baseline, clearly labeled as an AI estimate, not an official IELTS result, so you know exactly what you're working with. For now, read through the full framework below so that when you see your breakdown, you know exactly what it means and what to do about it.</p>
  <h2>How the IELTS writing band score is calculated</h2>
  <p>Before getting into what each criterion rewards, you need to understand the scoring math. Each writing task is marked separately on four criteria: Task Achievement (Task 1) or Task Response (Task 2), Coherence and Cohesion, Lexical Resource, and Grammatical Range and Accuracy. The four criterion scores are averaged to produce the band for that task. No single criterion carries more weight than another within a task, so a strong grammar score cannot rescue a weak Coherence and Cohesion score.</p>
  <p>What most candidates do not realize is that the overall writing band score is not a simple average of Task 1 and Task 2. Task 2 is weighted double. The formula adds your Task 1 band once and your Task 2 band twice, then divides by three. If you score 6.0 on Task 1 and 7.0 on Task 2, your overall Writing band is (6.0 + 7.0 + 7.0) / 3 = 6.67, which rounds to 6.5. This weighting is why spending 25 minutes on Task 1 and rushing Task 2 in the remaining 35 minutes is one of the most damaging strategic mistakes a candidate can make. Task 2 does more mathematical work than Task 1.</p>
  <p>The rounding rule shapes your final score in ways that matter at key admission thresholds. Averages below .25 round down, .25 to .74 round to the nearest half band, and .75 or above round up. A 6.625 average becomes 6.5; a 6.75 average becomes 7.0. Some institutions and immigration programs require exactly 7.0 in Writing, check your specific university or visa requirements, and in those cases the difference between a 6.74 and 6.75 combined average is the difference between meeting the requirement and retaking the test. That margin is worth knowing before you walk into the exam room.</p>
  <h2>Criterion 1: what Task Achievement and Task Response actually measure</h2>
  <p>This is the most commonly misunderstood criterion, partly because its name changes between tasks. In Task 1, it is called Task Achievement. In Task 2, it is called Task Response. The underlying question is the same in both cases: did you do what the task asked you to do?</p>
  <p>In Academic Task 1, the examiner checks whether you selected the key features of the graph or diagram, made relevant comparisons, and included a clear overview of the main trends. Listing every data point is not the goal; selecting and comparing the most significant ones is. In General Training Task 1, the focus shifts to whether the letter has a clear purpose, addresses every bullet point, and uses a tone appropriate to the situation. A formal letter written in an informal tone, or a friendly letter that reads like a business report, will lose marks here regardless of grammar quality.</p>
  <p>In Task 2, the examiner's primary question shifts from &quot;did you cover the features?&quot; to &quot;did you fully address the question and develop a clear position?&quot; The official Band 6 descriptor for Task Response states that the response &quot;addresses all parts of the task although some parts may be more fully covered than others.&quot; Band 7 requires that the response &quot;addresses all parts of the task&quot; with &quot;a clear position throughout.&quot; Band 8 asks for a &quot;well-developed response with relevant, extended and supported ideas.&quot; The gap between Band 6 and Band 7 is usually not dramatic language; it is more complete and consistent development of every part of the question.</p>
  <p><strong>Missing the overview in Academic Task 1 is the single error most likely to cap a Task Achievement score at Band 5, regardless of how accurate the data descriptions are.</strong> A practical approach used by many IELTS teachers is to write one or two sentences identifying the broadest trends from the visual data, without referring to specific figures, enough to orient the reader before the detail paragraphs. Band 8 does the same with greater precision and economy. If your Task 1 essay has no overview paragraph, fix that before working on anything else.</p>
  <h2>IELTS writing band score: Task 1 vs. Task 2 weighting and Criterion 2</h2>
  <h3>Criterion 2: coherence and cohesion, the invisible architecture of your essay</h3>
  <p>Coherence and Cohesion is the criterion most candidates underestimate. They assume it is about transition words, so they add &quot;Firstly,&quot; &quot;Furthermore,&quot; and &quot;In conclusion&quot; to every paragraph and expect the score to climb. It does not work that way. Coherence refers to how logically information is sequenced and how easy the essay is to follow as a whole. Cohesion refers to the devices that connect ideas: pronouns, conjunctions, reference words, and lexical chains. Using cohesive devices in a rote, formulaic way without genuine logical progression is itself a Band 6 marker, not a Band 7 one.</p>
  <p>The official Band 6 descriptor for Coherence and Cohesion notes that cohesion is present but &quot;may be faulty or mechanical.&quot; At Band 7, the reader follows the argument without effort, and paragraphing is clear and logical. At Band 8, cohesion is flexible and natural, and paragraph structure is consistent and purposeful. That word &quot;mechanical&quot; in the Band 6 descriptor matters: it describes an essay that uses the right linking words in the right places but still reads like a list rather than an argument.</p>
  <p><strong>Paragraph structure is a direct scoring factor under this criterion.</strong> Examiners look for a clear topic sentence, a developed explanation, and a relevant example or elaboration in each body paragraph. A single-block essay with no paragraph breaks is a reliable marker of Band 5 or lower. The paragraph skeleton, topic sentence, explanation, example, is a widely recommended teaching template that reflects what the Band 7 and Band 8 descriptors reward: clear sequencing, logical development, and consistent paragraphing. Candidates who rewrite their weakest body paragraph using this structure consistently see the most direct improvement in this criterion score.</p>
  <h2>Criterion 3: lexical resource and what vocabulary really means on the IELTS scale</h2>
  <p>The most persistent myth about this criterion is that it rewards complex vocabulary. Memorizing lists of obscure words and forcing them into your essay does not raise your Lexical Resource score. What the criterion actually rewards is a wide range of words used accurately and naturally, appropriate collocations, and a low frequency of errors. An uncommon word used incorrectly is worse for your score than a common word used precisely.</p>
  <p>The official descriptors make the progression clear. At Band 6, vocabulary is adequate but limited, and less common words may be inaccurate. At Band 7, there is sufficient range with some errors that do not impede communication. At Band 8, vocabulary is fluent and flexible, with only rare inaccuracies. Notice that Band 8 is defined by fluency and flexibility, not by difficulty. A candidate who uses &quot;demonstrate&quot; instead of &quot;show,&quot; &quot;beneficial&quot; instead of &quot;good,&quot; and &quot;implementation&quot; instead of &quot;use&quot;, while keeping those words accurate and naturally collocated, is writing at a higher lexical level than one who forces in &quot;perspicacious&quot; and &quot;ameliorate&quot; without control.</p>
  <p>Copying the exact wording of the question prompt throughout your essay is a direct signal of limited lexical range. Effective paraphrasing replaces prompt nouns and verbs with synonyms or grammatically transformed equivalents. For example, a question that asks about &quot;the increase in obesity rates among children&quot; can be paraphrased as &quot;rising levels of childhood overweight&quot; or &quot;the growing prevalence of excess weight in young people.&quot; These are not advanced phrases; they are accurate alternatives that show the examiner you have options. A Band 6 response tends to re-use &quot;increase in obesity rates&quot; throughout. A Band 7 response varies the phrasing across the essay, showing the examiner a genuine range of expression.</p>
  <h2>Criterion 4: grammatical range and accuracy, the balance examiners are looking for</h2>
  <p>This criterion rewards two things simultaneously: range and accuracy. A candidate who writes only grammatically correct simple sentences will not reach Band 7 under this criterion, regardless of how error-free those sentences are. Band 7 requires a mix of simple and complex structures used accurately. Band 8 requires a wide range of structures with the majority being error-free and only occasional minor errors present. The goal is controlled variety, not either extreme.</p>
  <p>Using complex structures incorrectly is more damaging than using a simple structure correctly. A relative clause with the wrong verb form, or a conditional that mixes its tenses, signals to the examiner that your grammatical range exceeds your grammatical control, a Band 5 to Band 6 characteristic. The safest path to Band 7 is to use complex structures you fully control: reported speech, relative clauses, passive voice, and conditional sentences, with enough accuracy that errors feel occasional rather than systematic.</p>
  <p>The <a href="https://ielts.idp.com/prepare/article-avoid-these-common-grammar-mistakes-in-ielts-writing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">grammar errors that appear most frequently</a> in Band 5 and Band 6 scripts are subject-verb agreement mismatches, article errors (a/an/the/zero), tense inconsistency across paragraphs, comma splices and run-on sentences, and preposition misuse. <strong>These are especially damaging when they recur throughout the essay rather than appearing once.</strong> An isolated tense error is a minor slip. Tense inconsistency in every paragraph is a systematic limitation that the Grammatical Range and Accuracy criterion will reflect directly.</p>
  <h2>Band 6, 7, and 8 essay paragraphs compared</h2>
  <p>Abstract descriptor language is useful as a framework, but seeing the criteria applied to actual writing makes the differences concrete. The following three paragraphs respond to the same Task 2 prompt: &quot;Some people believe that children spend too much time using technology. To what extent do you agree or disagree?&quot; Each paragraph represents one body paragraph from a full essay response.</p>
  <h3>Band 6 paragraph: what holds it back</h3>
  <p>Consider this excerpt: &quot;Firstly, technology is bad for children because they spend too much time on their phones. Children use technology every day and this is a problem. Technology can damage their health because they do not exercise. So it is clear that technology has a bad effect on children.&quot; This paragraph addresses the topic, but the idea stops at the surface. There is no explanation of the mechanism connecting screen time to health impact, no example, and no development beyond restating the same point in different words. The Coherence and Cohesion score takes a hit because &quot;Firstly&quot; signals a list, but the paragraph does not progress logically. Lexical Resource is limited because &quot;technology,&quot; &quot;bad,&quot; and &quot;children&quot; are repeated with no variation. Grammatical Range is restricted to simple sentences. Task Response is partial because the idea is present but underdeveloped.</p>
  <h3>Band 7 paragraph: what makes it work</h3>
  <p>Now consider this version: &quot;One significant consequence of excessive screen time is the reduction in physical activity among children. When young people spend their evenings watching videos or playing games, they are less likely to engage in outdoor activities or structured sport. A study of school-age children found that those who used devices for more than three hours daily were considerably less active than their peers. This pattern suggests that unrestricted access to technology may contribute to long-term health problems that extend beyond childhood.&quot; This paragraph develops one clear idea through explanation, evidence, and a forward-looking inference. The topic sentence is specific. The cohesion comes from logical progression, not formulaic connectors. Vocabulary is varied and accurate. Sentence structures are mixed. This is the type of development the Band 7 Task Response descriptor is measuring.</p>
  <h3>Band 8 paragraph: the features that distinguish top performance</h3>
  <p>A Band 8 version of the same idea might read: &quot;Excessive technology use displaces the physical activity that is essential to healthy childhood development. Children who spend multiple hours each day on devices are not simply choosing screens over sport; they are often substituting a passive, solitary activity for the peer interaction and physical challenge that build both fitness and social skills. Schools in several countries have reported measurable declines in playground engagement since the widespread adoption of personal devices, and pediatric health organizations have linked prolonged sedentary behavior to increased risk of early-onset metabolic conditions. The issue, therefore, is not technology itself but the absence of structured boundaries that would allow children to benefit from digital tools without sacrificing the activities that sustained attention and physical health require.&quot;</p>
  <p>Notice that Band 8 does not require perfection. It requires consistent, flexible control: precise collocations, a structured argument, embedded evidence, and sentence construction that varies naturally rather than by formula.</p>
  <h2>The errors that cost candidates the most bands and the drills that fix them</h2>
  <p>Based on the <a href="https://ielts.org/cdn/Guides/ielts-writing-band-descriptors.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official band descriptors</a> and common patterns in IELTS Writing performance, six errors consistently account for the most band loss. Ranked by impact across criteria, they are:</p>
  <ul><li><strong>Not answering all parts of the question</strong>, directly caps Task Response</li><li><strong>Missing the Task 1 overview</strong>, caps Task Achievement at Band 5</li><li><strong>Writing below the minimum word count</strong>, triggers an automatic penalty across criteria</li><li><strong>Weak paragraph structure with no clear topic sentences</strong>, reduces Coherence and Cohesion</li><li><strong>Persistent grammar errors recurring across the essay</strong>, limits Grammatical Range and Accuracy</li><li><strong>Limited vocabulary combined with prompt copying</strong>, reduces Lexical Resource</li></ul>
  <p>Each one harms a specific criterion directly, which is why fixing the right error first is more efficient than practicing writing in general.</p>
  <p>Each error has a targeted drill. For incomplete question coverage, use the prompt-parsing underline drill: before writing, underline every question part, instruction word, and sub-question, then write a one-line answer plan for each part. For missing Task 1 overviews, practice writing only the overview for ten different graphs in a row, aiming for one or two broad trends without specific figures, until the skill becomes automatic. For weak paragraph structure, use the paragraph skeleton exercise: rewrite any &quot;single block&quot; paragraph into a topic sentence, two developed supporting sentences, one example, and a closing sentence. For grammar errors, keep an error log of your top five recurring mistakes and rewrite one old paragraph per day fixing only those errors. For vocabulary repetition, take the prompt's key nouns and verbs and write three paraphrased versions before you start drafting.</p>
  <h2>A prioritized plan to raise your writing band score by 0.5 to 1.0</h2>
  <p>Most candidates assume their weakest area is vocabulary or grammar. In practice, the criterion most often holding candidates back from Band 7 is Task Response or Coherence and Cohesion, because those criteria operate at the structural level of the whole essay, not just at the sentence level. Improving vocabulary while the essay's argument structure remains underdeveloped is like repainting a car with a broken engine. Before any targeted drilling, you need a criterion-level baseline: which of your four scores is actually lowest?</p>
  <p>This is where IELTS Ai Pro gives you a concrete starting point. You can submit your own Task 1 or Task 2 essay and receive an instant AI-estimated band score across all four official criteria, the same framework this article has covered. Rather than guessing which criterion to work on, you see the breakdown directly and know exactly where to focus first. Use it as a directional tool alongside your own self-assessment and, where possible, feedback from a qualified teacher. That combined clarity is what turns a general &quot;I need to improve my writing&quot; intention into a specific, actionable plan. You can also use an IELTS writing band calculator or AI estimator to preview your score before committing to a full practice cycle.</p>
  <p>Once you have a baseline, follow this <a href="/blog/improve-your-ielts-writing-score-by-one-full-band">seven-step sequence</a> as a repeating cycle:</p>
  <ol><li>Submit a current essay to get your AI-estimated four-criterion baseline score.</li><li>Identify your lowest-scoring criterion and prioritize it above all others for the next two weeks.</li><li>Run the prompt-parsing drill before every new Task 2 attempt, without exception.</li><li>Practice Task 1 overviews separately until writing a brief overview from any graph takes under two minutes.</li><li>Rewrite one old paragraph per day targeting your top three recurring grammar errors from your error log.</li><li>Paraphrase the prompt's key vocabulary three ways before drafting each new essay.</li><li>Submit a new essay every two weeks to track your criterion-level progress and identify the next priority.</li></ol>
  <p>The seven steps are a cycle, not a checklist. Once your lowest criterion improves, a different criterion becomes the priority. Progress happens when you keep the loop running: write, get criterion-level feedback, identify the gap, run the targeted drill, write again. For many retakers, the 0.5 to 1.0 band improvement they need comes not from writing more essays but from writing with visibility into what is actually holding the score back.</p>
  <h2>Conclusion: improving your IELTS writing band score</h2>
  <p>Your IELTS writing band score is the average of four measurable criteria, each with clear descriptors for what good looks like at Band 6, 7, and 8. The math is transparent, the criteria are published, and the gap between where you are and where you need to be is narrower than most candidates realize. What is usually missing is not effort; it is the criterion-level visibility that tells you which specific gap to close first.</p>
  <p>The fastest path to a higher writing band is not practicing more. It is practicing with feedback that shows you exactly which of the four criteria is dragging your average down. Submit your essay to IELTS Ai Pro, see your criterion breakdown as an AI-estimated starting point, and use the framework in this article to read the results clearly. A focused, targeted approach to your weakest criterion will move your IELTS writing band score more efficiently than writing a full essay every day without knowing which part of it the examiner is penalizing.</p>
  <p>The 0.5 to 1.0 band improvement you are looking for is achievable. You now have the framework to pursue it systematically.</p>
