  $post$
  <p>Many guides on IELTS Writing Task 2 tell you to &quot;write a clear introduction&quot; and &quot;use cohesive devices.&quot; You already know that. What they rarely show you is exactly what a Band 7+ essay looks like sentence by sentence, how to plan under time pressure without freezing, and where your score is actually slipping without you realizing it. These IELTS Writing Task 2 tips close that gap, not with general advice, but with the real mechanics that separate Band 6 essays from Band 7+ ones.</p>
  <p>Every section here maps directly to those mechanics: reading the question correctly, structuring your argument, choosing vocabulary that signals precision, and avoiding the grammar traps that quietly cap scores at Band 6. Understanding these mechanics is the first step. The second step is writing practice essays and getting feedback scored against the same four criteria your examiner uses. By the end of this guide, you'll see exactly how to do both.</p>
  <p>Before anything else, though, you need to know what kind of essay you're being asked to write. That single skill addresses one of the most frequent reasons strong English speakers still score Band 5 or 6 on Task Response, and it takes under 60 seconds to apply.</p>
  <h2>IELTS Writing Task 2 Tips: Identify Your Question Type in Under 60 Seconds</h2>
  <p>The question type determines your position, your structure, and what counts as a complete answer. Misreading it means you could write a technically strong essay that doesn't address what the examiner was looking for, and Task Response automatically suffers. <a href="https://magoosh.com/ielts/ielts-writing-task-2-question-types/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Five question types</a> appear in Task 2, each with signal phrases that show up consistently across real exam prompts.</p>
  <p><strong>Opinion essays</strong> use &quot;To what extent do you agree or disagree?&quot; or &quot;Do you agree or disagree?&quot; These require a clear, single position that you maintain throughout the entire essay. Discussion essays use &quot;Discuss both views and give your opinion.&quot; Here, you present both sides fairly before stating your view. Advantages/Disadvantages prompts ask &quot;What are the advantages and disadvantages?&quot; or &quot;Do the advantages outweigh the disadvantages?&quot; Problem-Solution prompts ask &quot;What are the causes and what solutions can you suggest?&quot; Two-Part questions present two distinct questions about one topic, such as &quot;Why do people move to big cities? What problems can this cause?&quot;</p>
  <p>The required response changes significantly by type. An Opinion essay collapses if your position shifts between paragraphs. A Discussion essay fails Task Response if you only cover one side. A Two-Part essay suffers automatically if you answer one question and barely address the other. Knowing which type you're facing before you write a single word is non-negotiable.</p>
  <p>Build a 60-second pre-writing habit: underline the topic noun or phrase, circle the instruction verb (discuss, explain, to what extent), and decide whether the question asks for one position or two. That's it. This simple check eliminates misread questions and gives you a clear starting point for your outline.</p>
  <h2>The Four Criteria Your Examiner Uses to Calculate Your Band Score</h2>
  <p>Task 2 responses are marked on four equally weighted criteria, each worth 25% of your Task 2 score, as set out in the <a href="https://ielts.org/news-and-insights/ielts-writing-band-descriptors-and-key-assessment-criteria" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official IELTS band descriptors</a>. Understanding what each one means at Band 7 gives you a specific target rather than a vague instruction to write better.</p>
  <p><strong>Task Response</strong> at Band 7 means every part of the question is addressed, a clear position is present throughout, and main ideas are extended and supported, even if some are developed more fully than others. Coherence and Cohesion at Band 7 means ideas are logically organized with a range of cohesive devices used appropriately, though some over-use or under-use is acceptable.</p>
  <p>Here's what over-use actually looks like: &quot;However, the government should act. Moreover, individuals are also responsible. Furthermore, education is key. Nevertheless, laws are needed.&quot; Every sentence opens with a linking word. That's the pattern, and it reads mechanically to any examiner.</p>
  <p>Lexical Resource at Band 7 means a sufficient range of vocabulary that allows flexibility and precision, with only occasional errors in word choice or spelling. Grammatical Range and Accuracy at Band 7 means a variety of complex structures with frequent error-free sentences, though some errors are expected. Band 7 does not require perfection. It requires variety and general accuracy, not flawless writing.</p>
  <p>Understanding these criteria changes how you draft. When you know Coherence and Cohesion is 25% of your score, you stop treating linking words as an afterthought and start planning transitions at the outline stage. The rest of this article is, in effect, a <a href="/blog/improve-your-ielts-essay-score-the-10-step-guide">criterion-by-criterion improvement plan</a> built around these four scoring areas.</p>
  <h2>A Proven Essay Structure That Works for Every Question Type</h2>
  <p>Structure is the scaffold that holds your argument together. A clear, consistent structure directly supports your Task Response and Coherence scores because the examiner can follow your reasoning from the first sentence to the last.</p>
  <h3>Writing Your Introduction</h3>
  <p>A strong Task 2 introduction typically runs one to three sentences. A reliable approach uses two: the first paraphrases the prompt using synonyms, never copying the original wording; the second states your thesis, a direct answer to the question.</p>
  <p>Compare these two versions for a prompt about whether governments should legislate against food waste. Weak version: &quot;Food waste is a problem. In this essay, I will discuss both views.&quot; Band 7+ version: &quot;The question of whether authorities should introduce regulations on discarded food, or whether this responsibility lies with citizens, has become increasingly relevant. In my view, governments should lead by creating systemic solutions, while individuals must act within those frameworks.&quot; The second version paraphrases, takes a clear position, and signals the essay's direction without padding.</p>
  <h3>Building Body Paragraphs with PEEL</h3>
  <p>Body paragraphs follow the <a href="https://ielts.idp.com/vietnam/about/news-and-articles/article-peel-paragraph-ielts-writing/en-gb" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PEEL method</a>: Point, Explain, Example, Link. The Point states your main idea for that paragraph. The Explain sentence tells the reader why that idea is true or relevant. The Example grounds the argument in a specific, believable scenario. The Link connects back to the question or thesis.</p>
  <p>Here's what this looks like in practice for a Problem-Solution topic on traffic congestion: &quot;Investing in public transport is a more effective long-term response to urban congestion than road expansion. Rail and subway systems can move large numbers of passengers simultaneously, reducing the volume of private vehicles on city streets. Cities such as Seoul and Copenhagen, which have significantly expanded their metro networks, demonstrate measurable reductions in road crowding as a result of this investment. This confirms that funding mass transit is a sustainable approach to the congestion problem.&quot;</p>
  <p>Each paragraph develops one idea fully. Listing several ideas briefly in the same paragraph is a Band 6 pattern. This <a href="/blog/ielts-writing-task-2-essay-structure-band-7">PEEL approach</a>, widely used in IELTS model essays for Task 2, is what Band 7+ responses consistently demonstrate.</p>
  <h3>Writing Your Conclusion</h3>
  <p>Your conclusion restates the thesis in new words and summarizes the main argument in two to three sentences. It does not introduce new information, new examples, or vocabulary that hasn't appeared elsewhere in the essay. Adding a new idea in the conclusion creates a coherence problem: the examiner expects a summary, not an expansion, and the paragraph then lacks a clear function in the overall structure.</p>
  <h2>IELTS Writing Task 2 Tips: Your 40-Minute Time Plan</h2>
  <p>Time management is where Band 6 essays become Band 7 essays. A common issue among test-takers is poor time allocation, either rushing the planning phase and writing in circles for 40 minutes, or spending too long on one paragraph and never reaching the conclusion. The 5-30-5 framework, a widely recommended approach among IELTS preparation resources, addresses both problems directly.</p>
  <p><strong>Minutes 0 to 5</strong> are for planning only. Read and analyze the question, decide your position, and write a quick outline: one main point per body paragraph and one specific example per point. <strong>Minutes 5 to 35</strong> are for writing continuously, introduction, both body paragraphs, and the conclusion, without stopping to edit. <strong>Minutes 35 to 40</strong> are for proofreading: check grammar, word count (<a href="https://magoosh.com/ielts/ielts-how-many-words-to-write/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">minimum 250 words</a>), spelling, and punctuation only. Do not rewrite sentences during the checking phase.</p>
  <p>The planning phase is decision-making, not free-writing. Decide your thesis first. Then choose your two strongest points, not three, not four. For each point, identify one specific example you can write about confidently. Having these decisions made before you write prevents mid-essay hesitation, which destroys pacing and often leads to paragraph drift, where your body paragraph gradually stops being about your stated main point.</p>
  <p>The most common time-management mistake is editing while writing. Every minute you spend rephrasing a sentence during the writing phase is a minute you're not spending on reaching 250 words. If a word isn't coming to you, leave a blank and keep moving. Return only in the checking phase. This discipline separates test-takers who regularly finish under time pressure from those who run out of time with a half-written conclusion.</p>
  <h2>Academic Phrases That Signal Band 7+ Vocabulary</h2>
  <p>Lexical Resource is not about using the longest or rarest words. It's about using vocabulary with precision and flexibility. A reliable set of sentence frames gives you that flexibility without the risk of forcing a word you're not confident about.</p>
  <p>Organized by function, here are eight high-impact phrase starters for Task 2:</p>
  <ul><li>Introducing a main point: &quot;One significant factor is...&quot;</li><li>Adding detail or explanation: &quot;This is largely because...&quot;</li><li>Giving a specific example: &quot;To illustrate, consider the case of...&quot;</li><li>Making a concession before rebutting: &quot;While it is true that..., the evidence suggests...&quot;</li><li>Showing cause and result: &quot;As a result, ...&quot; / &quot;Consequently, ...&quot;</li><li>Introducing a contrasting view: &quot;On the other hand, ...&quot;</li><li>Adding supporting information: &quot;Furthermore, this is supported by...&quot;</li><li>Linking back to the thesis: &quot;This reinforces the view that...&quot;</li></ul>
  <p>These are flexible sentence frames, not memorized templates. IELTS examiners are trained to recognize formulaic, memorized openers, and according to official marking guidance, they do not improve your Lexical Resource score. What examiners reward is a writer who uses varied structures and vocabulary naturally across the whole essay.</p>
  <p>Cohesive devices deserve special attention. Linking adverbs like &quot;Furthermore,&quot; &quot;Nevertheless,&quot; and &quot;Consequently&quot; are useful, but they become a liability when overused. Repeating the same linking word, &quot;However&quot; or &quot;Moreover,&quot; for instance, several times in a single essay can sound mechanical and signal limited cohesion to the examiner. Natural cohesion in a Band 7+ essay also comes from referencing pronouns (&quot;this approach,&quot; &quot;these measures,&quot; &quot;such policies&quot;), relative clauses that add detail within a sentence, and synonym substitution so the same noun doesn't appear three times in one paragraph. Variety in cohesion signals a higher band; volume of transition words does not.</p>
  <h2>What a Band 7 Essay Does That a Band 6 Essay Doesn't</h2>
  <p>The difference between Band 6 and Band 7 is not dramatic, but it is consistent across all four criteria. Understanding exactly what changes gives you a practical self-evaluation tool.</p>
  <p>At Band 6, body paragraphs often list two or three ideas in short sentences without fully developing any of them. At Band 7, each paragraph builds one idea with explanation and an example. Using the food waste prompt as a direct comparison:</p>
  <p>Band 6 version: &quot;People waste a lot of food. This is bad for the environment. Governments should make laws to stop this. Individuals should also be responsible.&quot;</p>
  <p>Band 7 version: &quot;A substantial proportion of food waste originates not in households, but in the supply chain itself, where commercial standards cause edible produce to be discarded before it reaches consumers. Governments are uniquely positioned to address this structural problem by rewriting labelling laws and requiring large retailers to redirect surplus stock, creating a measurable impact on total waste levels.&quot;</p>
  <p>The Band 7 version is not longer for its own sake. It develops one idea with depth instead of addressing four ideas superficially.</p>
  <p>Vocabulary precision also shifts noticeably at Band 7. Common Band 6 choices include phrases like &quot;big problem,&quot; &quot;many people think,&quot; and &quot;this is very important.&quot; Band 7 upgrades these to &quot;a widespread concern,&quot; &quot;a substantial proportion of people argue,&quot; and &quot;a measurable impact.&quot; Here are five direct replacements worth studying before your exam:</p>
  <ul><li>&quot;Very big problem&quot; becomes &quot;a significant challenge&quot; or &quot;a pressing issue&quot;</li><li>&quot;Many people think&quot; becomes &quot;a growing number of people argue&quot; or &quot;it is widely held that&quot;</li><li>&quot;This is important&quot; becomes &quot;this has far-reaching implications&quot;</li><li>&quot;Bad for society&quot; becomes &quot;detrimental to social cohesion&quot;</li><li>&quot;The government should help&quot; becomes &quot;authorities are well-placed to address this&quot;</li></ul>
  <p>Run a quick four-question check before you stop writing: Did I answer every part of the question? Does each paragraph have one clear main idea? Did I avoid repeating the same words too often? Did I use at least three different sentence structures? This check takes under two minutes and reinforces the four scoring criteria without any external tools.</p>
  <h2>Grammar and Vocabulary Mistakes That Silently Pull Your Score Down</h2>
  <p>You can have a strong argument and a clear structure and still score Band 6 if grammatical accuracy is inconsistent. The errors below appear most often in Task 2 responses and reliably signal Band 6 rather than Band 7.</p>
  <h3>Grammar Errors Examiners Notice Most</h3>
  <ul><li><strong>Subject-verb agreement:</strong> &quot;The number of students have increased&quot; should be &quot;has increased&quot;</li><li><strong>Article misuse:</strong> &quot;Government must provide support&quot; should be &quot;The government must provide support&quot;</li><li><strong>Tense inconsistency:</strong> &quot;Many students study abroad and then returned home&quot; mixes present and past tense within the same clause</li><li><strong>Comma splices:</strong> &quot;Education is important, governments should fund it&quot; should be &quot;Education is important, so governments should fund it&quot;, or split into two sentences</li></ul>
  <p>Each of these errors appears frequently enough in Band 6 scripts that correcting them directly can shift your Grammatical Range and Accuracy score.</p>
  <h3>Vocabulary Habits That Cap Scores at Band 6</h3>
  <p>Word-form errors (&quot;this solution is economy&quot; instead of &quot;economical&quot;), wrong prepositions (&quot;worried on pollution&quot; instead of &quot;worried about pollution&quot;), overuse of &quot;very,&quot; &quot;extremely,&quot; and &quot;a lot,&quot; and informal register, words like &quot;kids,&quot; &quot;stuff,&quot; and &quot;get&quot; appearing in academic writing, all signal a limited Lexical Resource. Word repetition compounds the problem: using the same noun or verb more than twice in one paragraph tells the examiner your vocabulary range is restricted. Replace repeated nouns with demonstratives and synonyms: &quot;this approach,&quot; &quot;these measures,&quot; &quot;such initiatives.&quot;</p>
  <p>Here are four Band 6 sentences rewritten at Band 7 level:</p>
  <ul><li>&quot;Kids should get more stuff from schools&quot; becomes &quot;Children should receive greater resources and support from educational institutions.&quot;</li><li>&quot;The problem is very big for society&quot; becomes &quot;This issue has far-reaching consequences for social and economic stability.&quot;</li><li>&quot;People worried on climate change should act&quot; becomes &quot;Those concerned about climate change should take meaningful action.&quot;</li><li>&quot;It is very important that the government helps&quot; becomes &quot;It is essential that authorities intervene with targeted policy measures.&quot;</li></ul>
  <h2>From Knowing the Tips to Actually Executing Them</h2>
  <p>There's a real gap between understanding Task 2 strategies and applying them under timed conditions. Many test-takers read guides, recognize the logic, and still write Band 6 essays on exam day because they've never practiced the full process, from prompt to finished essay, with accurate, detailed feedback.</p>
  <p>Reading about cohesive devices doesn't tell you whether you're using them correctly in your own writing. Practicing without feedback creates a false sense of progress: you repeat the same patterns, assume they're working, and arrive at the exam with the same score as before. What closes the gap between effort and improvement is accurate feedback against the official four criteria, not general encouragement, and not a grammar checker that doesn't understand IELTS scoring.</p>
  <p>One option worth considering is <strong>IELTS Ai Pro</strong>, a platform designed specifically for this purpose. It lets you write practice Task 2 essays and receive AI-estimated band scores broken down across all four official criteria: Task Response, Coherence and Cohesion, Lexical Resource, and Grammatical Range and Accuracy. You also get inline corrections showing where your language fell short, and a progress dashboard that tracks score trends across multiple attempts. Keep in mind that AI scoring provides an estimate aligned with official criteria, it's a useful training tool, though scores may differ from those a human examiner would assign. A free plan is available, so you can start getting criterion-by-criterion feedback without any upfront cost.</p>
  <h2>Putting It All Together</h2>
  <p>You now have a complete system for Task 2. You can identify the question type in under 60 seconds, plan your argument in five minutes, structure body paragraphs using PEEL, and use vocabulary that signals precision and range. You also know the grammar patterns that quietly hold scores at Band 6 and how to evaluate your own writing against the same criteria your examiner applies.</p>
  <p>Reading tips creates awareness. Writing essays creates improvement. Take a Task 2 prompt from your next practice session, apply the 5-30-5 plan, write the full essay, time yourself, and run your self-check before you stop. If you want to know exactly where your essay falls short across all four criteria, IELTS Ai Pro can give you that criterion-by-criterion breakdown attempt after attempt.</p>
  <p>Use these <a href="/blog/ielts-writing-task-2-tips-for-a-reliable-band-7-score">IELTS Writing Task 2 tips</a> consistently and the path to Band 7+ stops being a mystery. It becomes a repeatable process: identify the question type, plan fast, develop one idea per paragraph with depth, and proofread with a clear checklist. Now you have that process. Use it.</p>
