  <p>You've written essay after essay. You've read model answers. You've watched YouTube videos about Task 2 structures. And yet your IELTS Writing score is stuck at Band 6, maybe 6.5. That's one of the most frustrating places to be in IELTS preparation, because the effort is real but the results don't reflect it. If you want to improve your IELTS writing band from 6 to 7, the missing piece isn't more essays, it's a structured, diagnosis-first plan that targets exactly where the official descriptors draw the line between those two bands.</p>
  <p>Here's the actual problem: you've been practicing without a clear picture of what Band 7 requires that Band 6 doesn't. Those two bands feel close, but the official band descriptors draw a sharp line between them. If you don't know where that line is, you can't cross it. More essays won't fix that. A targeted plan will.</p>
  <p>This article gives you an 8-week, week-by-week framework to raise your IELTS writing band from 6 to 7. By the end, you'll understand the four official criteria and exactly where the Band 6-to-7 gap lives, you'll have concrete drills for your weakest area, and you'll know how to track real progress across attempts so you walk into the exam with data, not hope.</p>
  <h2>1. What the Band 6 to Band 7 gap actually looks like</h2>
  <h3>The descriptor differences that matter most when you want to improve your IELTS writing band</h3>
  <p>The <a href="https://ielts.org/cdn/Guides/ielts-writing-band-descriptors.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official IELTS Writing band descriptors</a> assess four criteria: Task Response (or Task Achievement for Task 1), Coherence and Cohesion, Lexical Resource, and Grammatical Range and Accuracy. Many candidates assume the language and grammar criteria are where they need to focus hardest. A close reading of the official descriptors tells a different story.</p>
  <p>The wording for Lexical Resource and Grammatical Range and Accuracy is nearly identical across Bands 6, 7, and 8 in the official rubric. Both state that the writer &quot;uses a variety of complex structures,&quot; &quot;produces frequent error-free sentences,&quot; and &quot;uses less common lexical items with some awareness of style and collocation.&quot; The real separation between Band 6 and Band 7 happens in  <strong>Task Response and Coherence and Cohesion</strong>.</p>
  <p>At Band 6, Task Response reads: &quot;presents a relevant position although conclusions may become unclear or repetitive&quot; and &quot;presents relevant main ideas but some may be inadequately developed or unclear.&quot; At Band 7, it reads: &quot;presents a clear position throughout the response&quot; and &quot;presents, extends, and supports main ideas.&quot; That shift from &quot;may become unclear&quot; to &quot;clear throughout,&quot; and from &quot;inadequately developed&quot; to &quot;extended and supported,&quot; is your target. Those two phrases describe the entire gap.</p>
  <p>For Coherence and Cohesion, Band 6 says cohesion &quot;may be faulty or mechanical&quot; and paragraphing is &quot;not always logical.&quot; Band 7 says cohesive devices are used &quot;appropriately&quot; and there is &quot;a clear central topic within each paragraph.&quot; The goal is precision of structure, not just the presence of connectors. (You can verify all descriptor wording directly in the <a href="https://www.ielts.org/teaching-and-research/scoring-in-detail" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official IELTS Writing band descriptors</a>, available for both Task 1 and Task 2.)</p>
  <h3>Why most candidates misread their own work</h3>
  <p>Teachers who work with Band 6 candidates often observe that students believe their essays are on-topic and well-organized. From the inside, the ideas feel connected. From the examiner's perspective, the argument drifts, transitions feel like a checklist rather than logical movement, and paragraphs contain ideas rather than developed arguments. This blind spot is normal, which is why targeted diagnosis should come before any drilling starts.</p>
  <h2>2. Diagnosing where your writing score is leaking</h2>
  <h3>A four-criterion self-check for Band 6 writers</h3>
  <p>Before you write a single timed essay, you need to know which criterion is pulling your score down. Ask yourself one diagnostic question per criterion:</p>
  <ul><li><strong>Task Response:</strong> Does every body paragraph in your Task 2 essay directly answer the specific question asked, or does it address a related but slightly different idea?</li></ul>
  <ul><li><strong>Coherence and Cohesion:</strong> Can you explain in one sentence why the logical content of paragraph two follows from paragraph one, without relying on a connector word?</li></ul>
  <ul><li><strong>Lexical Resource:</strong> Count how many times the words &quot;good,&quot; &quot;bad,&quot; &quot;many people,&quot; or &quot;things&quot; appear in your last essay.</li></ul>
  <ul><li><strong>Grammatical Range and Accuracy:</strong> Read your last essay aloud and count how many sentences follow the same subject-verb-object pattern with no subordinate clauses.</li></ul>
  <p>Your honest answers to those four questions will identify your weakest criterion. That criterion gets priority for the first four weeks of the plan.</p>
  <h3>How to score your own writing before your first drill session</h3>
  <p>Take a recent essay you've written, print it or open it on screen, and annotate it against the Band 6 and Band 7 descriptor language from the previous section. Mark every paragraph where your main idea is not clearly extended. Highlight connector words that appear at the start of every paragraph like a sequence. Circle vague vocabulary. Flag any sentence where a comma joins two complete clauses without a conjunction. This exercise takes about 20 minutes and gives you a criterion-level picture of your current writing before any new practice begins.</p>
  <h2>3. Task relevance: the fix that moves scores fastest</h2>
  <h3>Why task response errors are the most punishing</h3>
  <p>Task Response is the first criterion for a reason. If your essay drifts off-topic, takes a vague position, or covers one part of the question fully and another only briefly, the examiner deducts marks before evaluating your language at all. A polished sentence written in the wrong direction is still a Band 6 sentence.</p>
  <p>Consider this Band 6-style Task 2 introduction: &quot;Some people think children should start school early but others think they should start after 7. I will discuss both sides and give my opinion.&quot; The position is present but unstated, and the framing is purely formulaic. Now compare this Band 7 version: &quot;The appropriate age for children to commence formal schooling remains a contentious issue. While early enrollment advocates cite cognitive development benefits, others argue children flourish more when formal education begins after age seven. This essay will examine both perspectives before presenting a clear position.&quot; The second version paraphrases naturally, states what the essay will argue, and signals control from the opening line. That difference in task framing sets the band from the first paragraph.</p>
  <h3>The &quot;question map&quot; technique for staying on track</h3>
  <p>Before drafting any Task 2 response, write one sentence summarizing exactly what the question is asking, then one sentence stating your specific position. Every body paragraph must connect back to both. This takes 60 seconds and eliminates the most common Task Response error: writing an essay that is vaguely about the topic rather than specifically about the question.  <strong>A Band 7 essay doesn't just address the theme; it addresses the question.</strong>  For Task 1, the same discipline applies: identify all the key features and write a clear overview before describing any specific data. An overview isn't optional at Band 7.</p>
  <h2>4. Coherence and cohesion: organizing for a higher band</h2>
  <h3>What Band 7 organization actually looks like</h3>
  <p>The descriptor language for Coherence and Cohesion at Band 7 is &quot;cohesive devices are used appropriately&quot; and &quot;each paragraph has a clear central topic.&quot; The critical word is &quot;appropriately.&quot; Band 6 writers typically use cohesive devices, but they use them mechanically: every paragraph opens with &quot;Firstly,&quot; &quot;Secondly,&quot; or &quot;Moreover,&quot; creating a list rather than an argument. The examiner sees this as a structural crutch, not as genuine organization.</p>
  <p>In a Band 7 essay, topic sentences carry the idea and connectors appear inside the reasoning to link back to a previous point logically. A paragraph that begins &quot;One direct consequence of this is...&quot; is more sophisticated than one that begins &quot;Moreover, there are benefits.&quot; The first requires the reader to understand what came before; the second could sit anywhere in the essay and mean nothing in particular.</p>
  <h3>Paragraph structure drills for cleaner organization</h3>
  <p>The <a href="https://ieltsetc.com/peel-paragraphs-ielts-writing-task-2/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PEEL method</a> (Point, Evidence, Explanation, Link) gives you a practical paragraph template for Band 7. Start with a topic sentence that states your main idea. Add a specific example or evidence. Explain in one or two sentences why that example supports your argument. End with a sentence that ties the paragraph back to the question or previews the next point logically. The explanation step is where most Band 6 paragraphs fall short: they have a point and an example but skip the analysis that shows the examiner you understand why the example matters.  <strong>Examiners check whether ideas develop within paragraphs, not just between them.</strong></p>
  <h2>5. Vocabulary and grammar upgrades that actually move the needle</h2>
  <h3>The specific errors that cost candidates half bands or more</h3>
  <p>The <a href="https://ielts.idp.com/prepare/article-avoid-these-common-grammar-mistakes-in-ielts-writing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">grammar errors</a> that appear most often in Band 6 scripts are article omission or misuse (a/an/the), subject-verb agreement failures with third-person singular forms, run-on sentences joined with only a comma (comma splices), and tense inconsistency within a single paragraph. The vocabulary errors are: overusing vague words like &quot;good,&quot; &quot;bad,&quot; or &quot;many people,&quot; repeating the same noun instead of paraphrasing, and using the wrong word form. Here's what those errors look like side by side with Band 7-level corrections:</p>
  <ul><li><strong>Article error:</strong> &quot;Education is key to success in life&quot; becomes &quot;A good education is key to lasting success.&quot;</li></ul>
  <ul><li><strong>Comma splice:</strong> &quot;The population is growing, governments must build more housing&quot; becomes &quot;The population is growing; therefore, governments must expand housing infrastructure.&quot;</li></ul>
  <ul><li><strong>Wrong word form:</strong> &quot;The economy has faced a difficulty situation&quot; becomes &quot;The economy faces a difficult situation.&quot;</li></ul>
  <ul><li><strong>Vague vocabulary:</strong> &quot;Many people think technology is good&quot; becomes &quot;A growing number of researchers argue that technology drives economic productivity.&quot;</li></ul>
  <ul><li><strong>Subject-verb agreement:</strong> &quot;The number of students have increased&quot; becomes &quot;The number of students has increased.&quot;</li></ul>
  <p>None of these fixes require advanced grammar knowledge. They require careful attention and deliberate practice reviewing your own sentences against a checklist.</p>
  <h3>How to build academic vocabulary without memorizing word lists</h3>
  <p>Isolated vocabulary lists don't help much. Collocations do. Instead of memorizing &quot;tackle,&quot; learn the full phrase: &quot;tackle a problem,&quot; &quot;tackle inequality,&quot; &quot;tackle environmental degradation.&quot; Instead of writing &quot;a big increase,&quot; write &quot;a significant increase&quot; or &quot;a sharp rise.&quot; When you learn a new academic word, study what it pairs with rather than what it means in isolation. This is the collocation method, and it's the difference between vocabulary that reads as deliberate and vocabulary that reads as forced. As a useful rule of thumb, five precise word swaps per essay, applied consistently, can shift your Lexical Resource impression without requiring you to overhaul your entire vocabulary range. Studying <a href="https://ielts.org/cdn/computer-delivered-sample-tests-academic-writing/ielts-academic-writing-example-responses-to-parts-1-and-2-with-band-scores-and-examiner-comments.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IELTS essay samples</a> at band 7 and band 8 is one of the best ways to see this collocation discipline in action.</p>
  <h2>6. Your 8-week plan to improve your IELTS writing band from 6 to 7</h2>
  <h3>Weeks 1 and 2: Diagnose, analyze, and rewrite</h3>
  <p>No timed full essays yet. In week one, mark two previous attempts against the band descriptor language from Section 1 and identify your weakest criterion. In week two, find a <a href="/blog/ielts-writing-task-2-tips-for-a-reliable-band-7-score">Band 7 Task 2 model essay</a> and annotate every paragraph: what is the main idea, how is it supported, where do cohesive devices appear, and what does the conclusion do? Then rewrite your week one essay with those observations applied. The goal is to internalize what Band 7 structure feels like before producing it under exam pressure. Plan for roughly 6 to 8 hours of focused work per week during this diagnostic phase, the analysis is slower than essay writing, but it builds the judgment you'll rely on in weeks 5 through 8.</p>
  <h3>Weeks 3 and 4: Targeted drills on your weakest criterion</h3>
  <p>Based on your self-diagnosis, spend two full weeks drilling the single criterion limiting your score. If Task Response is the problem, write five different thesis statements and sets of topic sentences for the same question before drafting a full essay. If Coherence and Cohesion is the gap, practice writing PEEL paragraphs in isolation, not full essays. If Lexical Resource is the issue, rewrite paragraphs replacing every vague word with a specific academic alternative. Keep each drill session to 30 to 45 minutes. Depth over volume matters here; one well-analyzed paragraph drill is worth more than three rushed full essays.</p>
  <h3>Weeks 5 and 6: Timed full essays with immediate review</h3>
  <p>Now introduce full timed practice: one Task 1 and one Task 2 per week under exam conditions (60 minutes total). After each attempt, apply the four-criterion self-check from Section 2, highlight your three most common errors, and write a corrected version of your weakest paragraph. Keep a running error log. The patterns you repeat across essays are your clearest signal for where to drill next.  <strong>The error log is one of the most useful tools in this plan</strong>: it turns individual essay feedback into a pattern you can see and act on.</p>
  <h3>Weeks 7 and 8: Mock conditions, score tracking, and final refinement</h3>
  <p>Increase to two full-length timed writing sessions per week. Compare your current essays against your week one attempts using the same four criteria. Refine the grammar and vocabulary patterns flagged in your error log. In week eight, complete a full IELTS Writing mock under timed conditions and evaluate the result against Band 7 descriptor language directly. This is your benchmark before exam day. If your estimated band across all four criteria is consistently at or above 7 across multiple attempts on different topics over the final two weeks, you have a reliable readiness signal.</p>
  <h2>7. Getting feedback that matches what examiners actually look for</h2>
  <h3>Why generic writing feedback doesn't move your IELTS score</h3>
  <p>Generic grammar tools catch spelling errors and surface-level grammar mistakes. But IELTS Writing is assessed on four specific criteria, and a red underline under a comma splice tells you nothing about whether your Task Response is focused, whether your paragraph structure meets Band 7 standards, or whether your cohesive devices are being used appropriately rather than mechanically. What moves your score is criterion-specific feedback: the kind that says &quot;your main argument shifts off-task in paragraph two&quot; or &quot;your cohesive devices appear only at paragraph openings and feel formulaic,&quot; not just &quot;grammar error found.&quot; This is exactly the gap that most free and low-cost prep tools leave open, they're built for general English writing, not for the four-criteria scoring logic that IELTS examiners apply. To increase your IELTS writing score, you need feedback that reflects how the exam is actually marked.</p>
  <h3>How IELTS Ai Pro gives you examiner-aligned feedback on every attempt</h3>
  <p>This is where IELTS Ai Pro fits naturally into the <a href="/blog/improve-your-ielts-writing-score-by-one-full-band">8-week plan</a>. After each timed writing session in weeks 5 through 8, submit your Task 1 or Task 2 response to IELTS Ai Pro's AI Writing evaluation. According to the platform, it returns an estimated band score across all four official IELTS criteria, with inline corrections showing exactly where language choices or structure fall short of the next band. The feedback is designed to mirror band descriptor logic: when it flags a Task Response issue, it explains why that paragraph doesn't fully address the question, not just that something is grammatically incorrect.</p>
  <p>Each submission is stored in your attempt history, giving you a trail of your actual band score trends over time. The progress analytics dashboard tracks your estimated scores by criterion across multiple attempts, so you can see whether your Coherence and Cohesion is consistently climbing even if your Lexical Resource score fluctuates week to week. That criterion-level data tells you whether to push forward or spend one more week drilling a specific area. IELTS Ai Pro also offers a free plan that includes Reading and Listening practice plus one monthly Writing evaluation, check the platform directly for current pricing and feature details, so you can start building that feedback history before committing to a paid tier.</p>
  <h2>8. How to know your writing is improving before exam day</h2>
  <h3>Reading your progress data, not just your essay scores</h3>
  <p>A single essay score can mislead you. A familiar topic might inflate your band temporarily, and an unfamiliar topic can lower it just as quickly. What actually confirms you're ready is a consistent trend across multiple attempts on different topics. When your Coherence and Cohesion score holds at Band 7 across several consecutive essays on unrelated subjects, that's not luck, that's a skill that's been built. Chasing individual scores is how candidates stay stuck; tracking criterion-level trends across attempts is how they move up.</p>
  <p>If you're using IELTS Ai Pro throughout weeks 5 to 8, your analytics dashboard will show you exactly that pattern. You'll see which criterion has improved the most, which is still inconsistent, and where to direct your final refinement work before exam day.</p>
  <h3>Your pre-exam self-check: five questions to ask before test day</h3>
  <p>Before you book your exam, answer these five questions honestly:</p>
  <ol><li>Does every body paragraph directly address the specific question asked, not just the general topic?</li></ol>
  <ol><li>Is your position stated clearly in the introduction and restated without contradiction in the conclusion?</li></ol>
  <ol><li>Can you explain the logical connection between each paragraph in one sentence, without using connector words as the explanation?</li></ol>
  <ol><li>Have you replaced the five words you overuse most with precise academic alternatives in your last three essays?</li></ol>
  <ol><li>Is the vast majority of your writing at the sentence level free of the recurring errors in your error log, substantially better than a rate where errors appear in every other sentence?</li></ol>
  <p>If the answer to all five is yes, and that answer is backed by two to three weeks of consistent feedback data across multiple timed attempts, you've done the preparation that reliably raises the IELTS writing band. The score will follow the work.</p>
  <h2>The full picture: what this plan actually asks of you</h2>
  <p>Raising your IELTS writing band from 6 to 7 isn't about volume. Candidates who improve fastest aren't the ones writing the most essays; they're the ones reviewing the right things after every attempt, drilling the criterion that limits them most, and tracking criterion-level trends rather than chasing individual scores. For many candidates, eight weeks of targeted, feedback-driven practice, roughly 6 to 8 hours of focused work per week, is enough to move the needle, provided the feedback is criterion-specific and the drilling stays disciplined. Results will vary based on starting level and how consistently you apply the plan, but the structure here is built around the preparation approach most likely to boost your IELTS writing band within that window.</p>
  <p>Start with the self-diagnosis in Section 2 before anything else. Identify your weakest criterion. Then follow the weekly schedule with the discipline to stay on one criterion at a time before moving to timed full essays. Use tools built around the official IELTS scoring criteria, including IELTS Ai Pro's criterion-specific AI feedback, to make sure the feedback you're acting on reflects what examiners actually assess.</p>
  <p>This 8-week plan is designed to improve your IELTS writing band from 6 to 7 through diagnosis, targeted drilling, and feedback-driven timed practice. The plan is here. Your next step is to open your last essay, run the four-criterion self-check from Section 2, and start with an honest picture of where you are right now.</p>
  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>How long does it take to improve your IELTS writing band from 6 to 7?</h3>
  <p>For many candidates, 6 to 8 weeks of structured, feedback-driven practice, at roughly 6 to 8 focused hours per week, is enough to produce a meaningful improvement. The timeline depends on your starting level, how consistently you apply criterion-targeted drills, and the quality of feedback you receive after each attempt. Candidates who combine diagnosis, targeted drilling, and timed practice with examiner-aligned feedback tend to see the fastest gains.</p>
  <h3>Which IELTS writing criterion matters most for moving from Band 6 to 7?</h3>
  <p>Task Response and Coherence and Cohesion are where the official band descriptors show the sharpest difference between Band 6 and Band 7. Most candidates focus on grammar and vocabulary, but the descriptor wording for Lexical Resource and Grammatical Range and Accuracy is nearly identical at Bands 6, 7, and 8. Fixing how clearly you answer the question and how logically your paragraphs develop will move your score faster than vocabulary drills alone.</p>
  <h3>Can I increase my IELTS writing score without a human tutor?</h3>
  <p>Yes, with the right tools and structure. The key is getting criterion-specific feedback on every attempt, feedback that maps to the official IELTS band descriptors rather than generic grammar correction. AI-powered platforms like IELTS Ai Pro are designed to provide exactly that kind of structured, examiner-aligned feedback at scale, making consistent practice more accessible for self-study candidates.</p>
