  <p>If you're asking what is the best free app to prepare for IELTS, you already know the frustration. You download something promising, work through the setup screen, tap &quot;Writing Practice,&quot; and hit a lock icon. Upgrade to Premium. Every useful feature, the feedback, the mock tests, the score breakdowns, is behind a paywall. Five minutes wasted, and you're back in the app store doing it all over again.</p>
  <p>The free IELTS app market is cluttered with tools that look useful in screenshots but deliver almost nothing without a subscription. Many of them give you a handful of sample questions, call that a &quot;free tier,&quot; and then charge you for anything that would actually help your band score. It's a frustrating pattern, and it burns time that you don't have when you're studying around a job, a class schedule, or a visa deadline.</p>
  <p>This article cuts through that. What follows is an honest look at which free IELTS apps actually deliver on mock tests, writing feedback, speaking practice, and score tracking, and which ones are just well-designed upsell funnels. One platform worth examining early is <strong><a href="/">IELTS Ai Pro</a></strong>, which claims to offer AI feedback across all four skills without requiring a credit card. We'll get into exactly what that means. But first, here's the framework for evaluating any IELTS study app you come across.</p>
  <h2>What a genuinely useful free IELTS app should actually include</h2>
  <p>Before naming any app, you need a clear filter. Most apps in the app store describe themselves with words like &quot;comprehensive,&quot; &quot;AI-powered,&quot; and &quot;exam-ready&quot; without any specifics. Those words mean nothing without understanding which features actually move the needle on your band score.</p>
  <h3>The four features worth measuring</h3>
  <p>A functional free IELTS practice app needs four things: timed mock tests, writing feedback, speaking practice, and progress tracking. Mock tests build time management and test familiarity, which are prerequisites for performing well under real exam conditions. Writing feedback tells you whether your Task 2 essay meets the official criteria, not just whether your grammar is passable. Speaking practice gives you a structured environment to record and review your responses. Progress tracking lets you see whether your band score is moving in the right direction over time, or whether you're stuck in place.</p>
  <p>If a free app is missing two or more of these pillars, it's a supplementary tool at best. You can use it to fill a specific gap, but you shouldn't treat it as your primary prep platform.</p>
  <h3>The difference between a free tier and a functional free tier</h3>
  <p>There's a meaningful difference between an app that offers five practice questions for free and one that lets you take a full timed mock, submit a writing response, and see criterion-level feedback without entering payment details. The first is a demo. The second is a study tool. A functional free tier, by a reasonable standard, is one where a dedicated candidate could practice consistently for 30 days and see measurable improvement without spending anything. Applied to the current IELTS prep app market, that bar is a high one, and most apps don't clear it.</p>
  <h3>Why feedback quality matters more than content volume</h3>
  <p>Fifty practice questions with no feedback is less valuable than five questions with detailed, criteria-aligned corrections. IELTS examiners score Writing responses across four official descriptors: Task Achievement, Coherence and Cohesion, Lexical Resource, and Grammatical Range and Accuracy. If your app gives you an overall score of 6.0 with no explanation, you have no idea whether you're losing points because your argument lacks development or because your paragraph transitions are weak. Both problems need different solutions. Feedback that identifies which descriptor is dragging your score down is the only kind that actually changes your studying behavior.</p>
  <h2>What Is the Best Free App to Prepare for IELTS? Start Here</h2>
  <p>IELTS Ai Pro positions itself as a platform for candidates who want honest, criteria-based feedback without paying to access it. According to the platform, the free plan exists as a permanent option, not a trial or a 7-day window, because candidates should be able to see their actual level before deciding to invest in more practice.</p>
  <h3>What the free plan reportedly covers</h3>
  <p>Per the platform's own description, the free tier includes <a href="https://www.ielts.international/ielts-practice-test" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cambridge-style Reading and Listening mock tests</a> with attempt history and answer review, one monthly Writing evaluation with an AI-estimated band score, and a basic analytics dashboard showing score trends over time. No payment is required to access these features. For candidates who are just starting out or retaking the exam and need to identify one or two weak areas, this free tier is worth testing from day one, verify the current feature set on the IELTS Ai Pro website before building your study schedule around it.</p>
  <h3>AI writing and speaking feedback that mirrors official scoring</h3>
  <p>The platform claims its Writing evaluation scores your response across all four official IELTS writing criteria, with inline corrections embedded directly in your text so you can see exactly where the feedback applies. The AI also generates an estimated band score for each criterion separately, not just an overall average. On the Speaking side, recorded responses are reportedly evaluated on fluency, vocabulary, grammar, and pronunciation, mapping to the official Speaking band descriptors. <strong>This level of multi-criteria feedback is typically locked behind a paid subscription on competing platforms</strong>, which makes a genuinely free version of it worth investigating if the claims hold up.</p>
  <h3>The adaptive study path and progress dashboard</h3>
  <p>IELTS Ai Pro claims to recommend your next activity based on your actual practice history and your target band score, rather than a generic course sequence that ignores your individual weaknesses. If your Listening score is consistently strong but your Writing criterion scores are uneven, the platform reportedly prioritizes Writing activities in your suggested path. The progress analytics dashboard is designed to track score trends by skill over time, so you can see whether your band is moving upward or plateauing. Most apps show you a raw score after each session and leave you to draw your own conclusions. The value of any adaptive platform is in whether it connects those scores to a clear next step.</p>
  <h2>Official apps from British Council and IDP: what's free and what isn't</h2>
  <p>The British Council and IDP apps carry the strongest credibility in IELTS prep because they are connected to the official test providers. That credibility matters. But it doesn't automatically mean these apps are the best tools for improving your band score, and their free tiers have real limits worth knowing before you rely on them.</p>
  <h3>British Council IELTS Prep app: content depth and its ceiling</h3>
  <p>The <a href="https://www.britishcouncil.co/en/english-exam/ielts/preparation/apps" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">British Council IELTS Prep app</a> offers a solid range of free content: timed Listening, Reading, Writing, and Speaking practice, vocabulary exercises, grammar drills, videos of actual IELTS speaking interviews, a preparation checklist, tips aligned to official assessment criteria, and a countdown to your test date. The newer IELTS Ready experience from British Council also includes a free Express Mock and Mini Mock with some AI feedback attached. These are genuine strengths, and the practice material is as close to official quality as you'll find outside of Cambridge materials.</p>
  <p>The ceiling, however, is real. The free tier does not give you deep writing correction across all four official criteria or a per-criterion band score for your speaking responses. The Premium layer unlocks 40 full scored practice tests, expanded AI feedback, full-skill courses, and live expert webinars. If you need diagnostic feedback that tells you specifically why your Writing score is stuck at 6.0, the free British Council app doesn't get you there.</p>
  <h3>IELTS by IDP: mock tests and score estimation</h3>
  <p>The IELTS by IDP app includes 80 or more free mock tests described as reflecting the latest exam format for both Academic and General Training. The app also offers auto-marked Listening and Reading practice with immediate feedback and some score estimation capability. IDP's official-provider status gives it credibility that third-party apps simply cannot match on content authenticity. For candidates who want high-quality practice material in a format they can trust, this is a strong option.</p>
  <p>One thing worth knowing: <a href="https://ielts.idp.com/indonesia/prepare/ielts-sample-test/en-gb" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IDP's free computer-based familiarization test</a> is untimed, which means it doesn't replicate the pressure of real exam conditions. For full timed simulation, you need to look at IDP's paid mock tests or supplement with another platform. The IDP app is also not built around diagnosing your weaknesses systematically over time.</p>
  <h3>Where official apps fall short for serious score improvement</h3>
  <p>Official apps are excellent for authentic practice material and for getting comfortable with real exam question types. They are not built to diagnose your weaknesses or explain why your Writing Task 2 is scoring a 6.0 instead of a 7.0. The gap between &quot;I practiced&quot; and &quot;I improved&quot; requires feedback that maps directly to official descriptors and tells you what to change. Using official apps alongside a feedback-focused platform makes more sense than relying on either one alone, which is the logic behind the two-app combination covered later in this article.</p>
  <h2>Apps that advertise free but lock the useful features away</h2>
  <p>Some apps have built their entire marketing around words like &quot;free&quot; and &quot;AI-powered&quot; while quietly placing everything that would actually help your score behind a paid plan. Knowing which apps fit this pattern saves you the time of downloading, setting up an account, and discovering the limitations yourself.</p>
  <h3>IELTS Test Pro: what you get without paying</h3>
  <p><a href="https://ielts-testpro.com/get-pro/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IELTS Test Pro</a>'s free tier is more generous than most. It includes over 3,000 practice questions, flashcards, offline mode, text-to-speech support, a daily review calendar, and basic score reports with analytics. That's a meaningful content library, and for reading and listening practice volume, it holds up. The paywall, though, covers everything that makes the app diagnostic: the AI-powered scoring tool, the smart study roadmap, detailed answer explanations, writing and speaking model answers, full writing history, and the simulation tests. If you want IELTS Test Pro to tell you not just whether your answer was right but why, and what to do about it, you need the Pro subscription.</p>
  <h3>Magoosh: a subscription-first model dressed as free prep</h3>
  <p>Magoosh's IELTS product is primarily a paid subscription platform. The free content functions as a preview rather than a functional study environment. Unlimited practice, AI feedback, full-length mock tests, and targeted diagnostics are all part of the paid offering. The paid plans do include substantial content: video lessons, hundreds of practice questions, multiple full mock tests, and on higher-tier plans, human-graded writing feedback. If you need a free IELTS study app, Magoosh doesn't fit that requirement. It belongs on a different list.</p>
  <h3>Red flags that tell you a free app isn't actually free</h3>
  <p>Before downloading any IELTS prep app free download, watch for these warning signs. If writing or speaking feedback requires spending a &quot;credit&quot; that resets on a paid cycle, the feature is paywalled regardless of what the app store description says. If mock tests require starting a premium trial to complete, the app is not offering free tests. And if your score report after a practice session is blurred or shows only a partial breakdown labeled &quot;upgrade to see more,&quot; the analytics aren't free. These signals appear quickly once you know to look for them, and they're worth identifying before you invest time setting up an account.</p>
  <h2>Speaking and writing feedback on free apps: what's real and what isn't</h2>
  <p>Writing and Speaking are the two skills most candidates struggle with, and they're also the two skills where free apps are most likely to disappoint. Recognizing what &quot;feedback&quot; actually means across different apps helps you avoid wasting time on tools that give you a number without any direction.</p>
  <h3>The landscape of free AI speaking feedback</h3>
  <p>Apps like SoundPerfect and SpeakShark do offer AI-based speaking feedback on their free tiers, including pronunciation accuracy, fluency scoring, and some vocabulary-level assessment. These tools are worth exploring if pronunciation is your weakest point. The gap, however, is that IELTS-specific speaking feedback requires scoring against the four official IELTS Speaking descriptors: Fluency and Coherence, Lexical Resource, Grammatical Range and Accuracy, and Pronunciation. Most free pronunciation apps score phoneme accuracy, not discourse organization. <strong>Feedback that doesn't map to official IELTS criteria won't tell you what an examiner would actually deduct points for.</strong></p>
  <h3>Writing evaluation: what &quot;free&quot; usually means in practice</h3>
  <p>When most apps advertise free writing feedback, they mean an overall score with a single paragraph of generic commentary. You might get &quot;Your grammar needs improvement&quot; or &quot;Good use of vocabulary&quot; with no specific examples and no breakdown by criterion. That type of feedback doesn't help you understand whether your low score comes from weak topic development, poor paragraph cohesion, limited vocabulary range, or recurring grammar errors. An IELTS practice app that breaks down scores by all four official criteria and embeds corrections directly in your response text gives you something actionable, you can see exactly which sentence triggered the feedback and why.</p>
  <h3>Why criterion-aligned feedback is the only kind that changes your score</h3>
  <p>A score of 6.5 with no explanation is useless to a retaker who needs a 7.0. What that candidate needs is a clear signal: are they losing points on Task Achievement because their argument isn't fully developed, or on Coherence and Cohesion because their body paragraphs lack clear central ideas? These are different problems with different solutions. Criterion-aligned feedback forces you to study the right thing, the only way to raise your band score rather than just practice the same way you always have and hope for a different result.</p>
  <h2>Progress tracking and band score accuracy in free apps</h2>
  <p>Candidates who use multiple apps often end up with conflicting score estimates and no clear sense of their actual level. Understanding how to read progress data, and which apps to trust for it, prevents you from making study decisions based on unreliable numbers.</p>
  <h3>Which free apps track your performance over time</h3>
  <p>IELTS Ai Pro, IELTS Test Pro, and several third-party platforms offer some form of progress dashboard on the free tier. Good progress tracking includes skill-level breakdowns by section, attempt history across multiple sessions, score trend lines that show movement over weeks rather than just one session, and recommendations tied to your weakest areas. A single post-session score with no history is not progress tracking, it's a receipt. The difference matters because you need trend data to know whether your studying is actually working.</p>
  <h3>How reliable are AI band score estimates?</h3>
  <p>No third-party AI scoring system is officially validated by Cambridge or the British Council. The most trustworthy apps label their estimates as practice scores rather than official predictions; some, like IELTS MySpeaking, explicitly state that their band scores are not official score predictions. Informal comparisons between AI writing scoring tools and human examiner benchmarks suggest that well-calibrated, IELTS-specific tools can land within roughly 0.5 bands of a human examiner score for writing tasks, while general-purpose AI tools can be off by 0.5 to 1.5 bands and tend toward generosity. Use AI scores to track your own direction over time, not to predict your real test result.</p>
  <h3>Using analytics to make smarter study decisions</h3>
  <p>Your progress data is most useful when it tells you where to focus next. If your Reading score is consistently sitting at 7.5 while your Writing score hasn't moved from 6.0 in three sessions, that's a clear signal: your remaining study time belongs in Writing. An adaptive IELTS study app builds this logic into its activity recommendations, connecting your score history to your next suggested task. Most apps record what you did. The best ones use that history to decide what you should do next.</p>
  <h2>How to Choose the Best Free App to Prepare for IELTS: The Two-App Combination</h2>
  <p>You don't need five apps, a premium subscription, or a tutoring package to cover all four IELTS skills at zero cost. Two apps, used consistently, can get you there.</p>
  <h3>IELTS Ai Pro plus British Council: the core combination</h3>
  <p>The suggested approach is to use IELTS Ai Pro as your primary platform for Writing evaluation, Speaking practice, and progress tracking, with criterion-based AI feedback, Cambridge-style mock tests, and score analytics, while layering the British Council IELTS Prep app on top for vocabulary exercises, grammar drills, authentic speaking interview videos, and supplementary Reading and Listening content. Together, these two free tools aim to cover every skill tested in the IELTS exam. The British Council brings official-quality practice material; IELTS Ai Pro is designed to bring the diagnostic feedback layer that turns practice into improvement. Confirm the current free-tier limits of both apps before committing to this combination as your primary study stack.</p>
  <h3>A weekly free study schedule built around these tools</h3>
  <p>A realistic schedule for a working adult with 45 to 60 minutes per day looks like this:</p>
  <ul><li>Two Reading or Listening mock tests on your chosen IELTS practice app, with answer review after each session</li><li>One Writing Task 2 submission for AI feedback, reviewed against the criterion breakdown</li><li>One Speaking practice session recorded and evaluated against official descriptors</li><li>Two British Council vocabulary or grammar sessions to reinforce lexical resource and grammatical range</li></ul>
  <p>That's five study sessions per week, each under an hour, covering all four skills. It's a schedule designed for consistency, not heroics. Consistency over four to six weeks shows up in your score trend data in a way that cramming never does.</p>
  <h3>When a third app makes sense and when to consider upgrading</h3>
  <p>If pronunciation is your single biggest weakness, adding a free pronunciation-focused app like SoundPerfect gives you targeted speaking practice on top of your core IELTS speaking evaluation. That's a sensible third tool for a specific gap. For most candidates, though, the two-app combination is enough to work with for the first 30 days. After 30 days, look at your score trend data. If your band in Writing is still 0.5 or more below your target and your test date is approaching, that's the moment to consider upgrading to unlimited Writing evaluations. The free stack gives you the data to make that decision wisely rather than guessing.</p>
  <h2>Start where you actually are, not where you hope you are</h2>
  <p>So, what is the best free app to prepare for IELTS in 2026? For most candidates who want meaningful, criteria-aligned AI feedback without paying upfront, IELTS Ai Pro is the clearest starting point, according to its own feature claims, it is among the free platforms that evaluates Writing against all four official descriptors, scores Speaking responses on official criteria, and connects both to a progress dashboard that guides your next step. Verify those features directly on the platform before building your schedule around them.</p>
  <p>Paired with the British Council IELTS Prep app for vocabulary, grammar, and authentic practice content, you have a complete, free study system that covers every section of the exam. The combination works because each tool does what the other doesn't. British Council gives you breadth and official credibility. A feedback-focused IELTS study app gives you diagnostic depth.</p>
  <p>Take one practice test on IELTS Ai Pro today, even just a Reading mock. Look at your score. Then submit a Writing Task 2 and read the criterion breakdown. You'll know within one session exactly where your band stands and which descriptor is holding it back. That clarity is worth more than another week of downloading IELTS prep apps free of charge that don't tell you anything useful.</p>
